# A2 — autosend-bridge-telegram → packages/queue : satisfy the single-write-path rule
#
# RULES VIOLATED  R3 (MONOREPO §3): "Only `spine` writes `decision`,
#                 `outcome_event`, `holdout_assignment`; only `harness` executes."
#
# EVIDENCE        packages/utilities/autosend-bridge-telegram/src/decisions-postgres.ts
#                   :132  INSERT INTO decision_log (tenant_slug, agent_name, ...)
#                         — inside RECORD_DECISION_SQL, the package's write path
#                   :46   execFile("psql", ...) — the reader
#                   :63   FROM decision_log — inside FETCH_SQL
#
# WHAT CHANGES    Nothing is rewritten. One file RELOCATES to packages/spine, two
#                 re-exports drop from the queue's public surface, and two CLI
#                 entrypoints repoint.
#
# WHY IT IS SAFE  The seam already exists, same as A1. `bridge.ts` consumes
#                 `DecisionSource` as an injected interface from ./types.js and
#                 never touches Postgres. `proposeApproval` and
#                 `awaitApprovalDecision` are unchanged. Only the concrete
#                 Postgres implementation moves, and it moves to the package that
#                 the rule says owns those rows.
#
#                 The storage design is already spine-shaped and the source says
#                 so at :6-15: approval decisions are recorded as ordinary
#                 `decision_log` rows rather than a separate `approvals` table,
#                 "to keep the D1-B audit chain inside the same append-only
#                 substrate". That is the Value Spine substrate by another name.
#
#                 First-valid-reply-wins is preserved verbatim: the NOT EXISTS
#                 guard at :93-100 travels inside the SQL constant, untouched.
#                 production-wiring.test.ts:160-163 asserts on that guard and on
#                 the RLS scoping, so the property is regression-tested wherever
#                 the constant lands.
#
# ⚠ RULE GAP      R3 names only WRITES. Neither R2 nor R3 explicitly forbids
#                 READING `decision_log` from outside spine — R2 is scoped to
#                 "brain content", and decision_log is spine content, not brain
#                 content. This patch moves the reader (FETCH_SQL) as well as the
#                 writer, on the §4 reading that the Value Spine tables are
#                 "P2 writes, ledger measures, P4 reads" — i.e. spine owns both
#                 directions. THIS IS AN INTERPRETATION, NOT A QUOTED RULE.
#                 Logged for founder confirmation; if the ruling goes the other
#                 way the reader stays in queue and this patch shrinks.
#
# VERIFY          pnpm --filter @core/autosend-bridge-telegram test
#                 CortexOS baseline: 34 passed (34).
